@page "/docs/troubleshooting/onclick"

@* OnClick, @bind reactions and other C# events need an interactive page; on a static one a button
   renders type="button" and nothing runs. Fixes: a Submit button in a form (Name/Value say which),
   a link, or an interactive page. Checked in a scratch page; button-multi-submit in all modes. *@

<ArticlePage Section="troubleshooting" Slug="onclick">
    <Lead>
        A static page has no connection to the server once it's loaded, so C# can't run on a click. Post a form or follow
        a link instead, or make the page interactive.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">Problem</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            A button with <Code>OnClick</Code> does nothing on a static page: no error, no request, and the page doesn't
            change. The same goes for other C# events, such as a menu item's <Code>OnClick</Code> or a tag's
            <Code>OnRemove</Code>, and for code that runs when a bound value changes (<Code>ValueChanged</Code>,
            <Code>@@bind-Value:after</Code>).
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="razor" Title="Post.razor">@Broken</Code>

        <Heading Level="2">Cause</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            C# event handlers run over an interactive page's circuit (or in WebAssembly). A static page renders once per
            request and sends HTML, with nothing left on the server to call, so the button renders as a plain
            <Code>&lt;button type="button"&gt;</Code>. The API pages label these parameters <strong>Interactive only</strong>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Fix</Heading>
        <TextBlock>Pick by what the click should do:</TextBlock>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Change data:</strong> make it a <Code>Submit</Code> button in a <Code>Form</Code>. On a static page
                the post runs <Code>OnValidSubmit</Code>; on an interactive page the same handler runs without a post. With
                several buttons, give each a <Code>Name</Code> and <Code>Value</Code>, which post with the form on a static
                page, and an <Code>OnClick</Code> for interactive pages, where nothing posts.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Show something else:</strong> make it a link with <Code>Href</Code>, carrying any choice in the query
                string.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>React to every change:</strong> make the page interactive with <Code>@@rendermode InteractiveServer</Code>
                (or WebAssembly or Auto). For filters, <Code>FilterForm</Code> applies them through the address, on a
                static page too.
            </ListItem>
        </List>
        <Code Block Language="razor" Title="Post.razor" HighlightLines="3-6,14-15,18,24">@Fixed</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            Here each button posts its <Code>intent</Code> on a static page, and <Code>OnClick</Code> records it on an
            interactive one:
        </TextBlock>
        <ExampleViewer Example="button-multi-submit" ModeSwitch />

        <Heading Level="2">Related</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/components/button">Button</Anchor>: submit buttons, links and <Code>OnClick</Code>.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">Render modes</Anchor>: choosing a page's mode, and state on static pages.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/troubleshooting/control-named-action">A form posts to [object RadioNodeList]</Anchor>: names to avoid for a button.</ListItem>
        </List>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    private const string Broken = """
        <Button OnClick="PublishAsync">Publish</Button>
        """;

    private const string Fixed = """
        <Form Model="Model" FormName="post" OnValidSubmit="SaveAsync">
            <TextField @bind-Value="Model!.Title" />
            <Button Submit Name="intent" Value="publish"
                    OnClick='() => _pressed = "publish"'>Publish</Button>
            <Button Submit Name="intent" Value="draft"
                    OnClick='() => _pressed = "draft"'>Save draft</Button>
        </Form>

        @code {
            [SupplyParameterFromForm(FormName = "post")]
            private PostDraft? Model { get; set; }

            // Static pages: the pressed button posts its Name and Value.
            [SupplyParameterFromForm(FormName = "post", Name = "intent")]
            private string? Intent { get; set; }

            // Interactive pages don't post: OnClick says which button it was.
            private string? _pressed;

            protected override void OnInitialized() => Model ??= new();

            private async Task SaveAsync()
            {
                var publish = (Intent ?? _pressed) == "publish";
                await Posts.SaveAsync(Model!, publish);
            }
        }
        """;
}
